import{_ as s,o as t,c as o,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.D7TN4pt9.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Enrolling a host for cross-frontend access","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/enrolling.md","filePath":"guide/enrolling.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"guide/enrolling.md"};function a(h,e,d,r,l,c){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="Enrolling-a-host-for-cross-frontend-access" tabindex="-1">Enrolling a host for cross-frontend access <a class="header-anchor" href="#Enrolling-a-host-for-cross-frontend-access" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Enrolling a host for cross-frontend access {#Enrolling-a-host-for-cross-frontend-access}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A box is <strong>enrolled</strong> when any frontend in the fleet can select its rows, attach to one, and type — without that frontend having an account, a key, or a route to the box. The route is: frontend → its own <code>ssh</code> child to the hub → the <strong>hub</strong>&#39;s socket for that box → one <code>ssh</code> from the hub → <code>sotd stdio-bridge</code> on the box → that box&#39;s daemon. The hub is the only machine that ever ssh&#39;s in. The <code>hosts.toml</code> grammar is under &quot;hosts.toml&quot; in Configuration files; this page is the procedure.</p><p>Everything here is run by an operator, once per box. Nothing in it is automatic, and every step has a check that fails loudly — &quot;it looked right&quot; is not one of them.</p><h2 id="What-the-hub-ends-up-holding" tabindex="-1">What the hub ends up holding <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-the-hub-ends-up-holding" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the hub ends up holding {#What-the-hub-ends-up-holding}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The hub must be a Linux host running <code>systemd --user</code>: every artifact below is a user unit, and <code>apply</code> and <code>refresh</code> call <code>systemctl</code>. <code>sotd topology apply --yes</code>, run on the hub, writes and enables <strong>one listener per enrolled host</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">File (in <code>~/.config/systemd/user/</code>)</th><th style="text-align:right;">What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;.socket</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the listener, at the path <code>sotd topology relay-sockets</code> prints; <code>Accept=yes</code>, <code>SocketMode=0600</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;@.service</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">one instance per accepted connection, the connection as its stdin/stdout</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;@.service.d/zz-sot-relay-command.conf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the command: <code>ssh … &lt;host&gt; sotd stdio-bridge</code> (no argument: that box&#39;s own daemon), set last among the files of that directory</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;.socket.d/topology.conf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the host pin <code>ConditionHost=|&lt;hub&gt;</code> and <code>ConditionHost=|&lt;hub&gt;.*</code>: a home the fleet shares shows the unit to every host, so it must start on the hub only</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two more pins are not per host: <code>sot-relay-tunnel@.service.d/topology.conf</code> starts every reverse tunnel on the hub only, and <code>sotd.service.d/topology.conf</code> starts the daemon only on the hosts declared <code>daemon</code> or <code>frontend</code>.</p><p>All four files and both pins are <strong>generated by the hub&#39;s daemon</strong>: when it runs as <code>sotd.service</code> it rewrites them at each start (a daemon started by hand rewrites nothing until <code>sotd topology refresh</code>) whenever their text differs from what the running <code>sotd</code> generates, and <code>sotd topology refresh</code> does the same by hand. Each start also reloads systemd and restarts every enabled relay socket, so a step that failed at one start is redone at the next; a running bridge is not interrupted. A hand edit to them is lost. A per-host override is an <code>Environment=</code> line in a drop-in of its own beside the service (any name but <code>topology.conf</code>, which is apply&#39;s own). The command is the hub&#39;s: systemd applies drop-ins in file-name order, so the hub&#39;s drop-in sets the command last among the files of the hub&#39;s own unit directory, and an <code>ExecStart=</code> in an earlier-sorting one has no effect. Name your own <code>Environment=</code> drop-ins so they sort before <code>zz-sot-relay-command.conf</code> (for example <code>local.conf</code>): refresh renames any <code>.conf</code> there that sorts after it to <code>.conf.retired</code>, and says so. A drop-in in another systemd directory that overrides the command is reported by refresh as a failure, not fixed.</p><div class="language-ini vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ini</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># ~/.config/systemd/user/sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;@.service.d/local.conf</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">[Service]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># not on the ssh PATH:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">Environment</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SOT_RELAY_SOTD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=/home/&lt;user&gt;/.local/share/sot/bin/sotd</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># alias differs from the declared host name:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">Environment</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SOT_RELAY_TARGET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=&lt;ssh alias&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>For a Windows host, ssh starts <code>cmd.exe</code> in the profile folder and the Windows install does not put <code>sotd</code> on <code>PATH</code>, so give its path in that host&#39;s own drop-in:</p><div class="language-ini vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ini</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">[Service]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">Environment</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SOT_RELAY_SOTD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">=AppData\\\\Local\\\\sot\\\\bin\\\\sotd.exe</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The host sits in the unit&#39;s <strong>name</strong>, not after an <code>@</code>, because a templated socket with <code>Accept=yes</code> hands its service the connection id and not the socket&#39;s instance (systemd issue #19071) — a per-host prefix is the only spelling in which the service knows which box to ssh to.</p><h2 id="The-checklist" tabindex="-1">The checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The checklist {#The-checklist}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each step&#39;s check must pass before the next one is worth doing.</p><ol><li><p><strong>ssh, from the hub, non-interactively.</strong> <code>ssh -o BatchMode=yes &lt;host&gt; sotd --version</code> prints a version. A prompt, a host-key question or a &quot;command not found&quot; here is the whole enrollment failing early: nothing downstream runs attended. If <code>sotd</code> is found interactively but not this way, note its full path for the <code>SOT_RELAY_SOTD</code> override above rather than editing shell profiles.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>sotd</code> runs as a user service on the box</strong>, started by the OS, not by a launcher, surviving with nobody logged in. On the box: <code>systemctl --user show -p WantedBy --value sotd.service</code> (it names <code>default.target</code> where the boot drop-in is loaded; <code>is-enabled</code> prints <code>static</code> on every host, so it checks nothing), <code>systemctl --user cat sotd.service</code> (its pin drop-in names this host), <code>loginctl enable-linger</code> where that applies, and <code>sotd status</code> answers. An instrument box wants this regardless of anything on this page.</p></li><li><p><strong>The bridge answers.</strong> From the hub, send one hello frame through <code>ssh -o BatchMode=yes &lt;host&gt; sotd stdio-bridge</code> and get the daemon&#39;s own reply, byte for byte, with nothing else on stdout. The bridge closes when its input ends, so keep the input open for a few seconds:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">proto</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;$(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -o</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> BatchMode=yes </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">host</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> sotd </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">--version</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -oE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;protocol [0-9]+&#39; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">|</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -oE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;[0-9]+&#39;)&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">uid</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;$(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -o</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> BatchMode=yes </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">host</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> id </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">-u</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">)&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{ </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">printf</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;v&quot;:1,&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;kind&quot;:&quot;req&quot;,&quot;op&quot;:&quot;hello&quot;,&quot;payload&quot;:{&quot;client_id&quot;:&quot;enrol-check&quot;,&quot;last_seen_revision&quot;:0,&quot;protocol&quot;:%s,&quot;app_version&quot;:&quot;enrol-check&quot;,&quot;host&quot;:&quot;enrol-check&quot;,&quot;os_user&quot;:&quot;uid:%s&quot;}}\\n&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$proto</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$uid</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sleep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; } </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">  |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -o</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> BatchMode=yes</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">hos</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> sotd</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> stdio-bridge</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A good reply is one JSON line whose <code>payload</code> carries the daemon&#39;s <code>app_version</code> and <code>host</code>. With the daemon stopped, the same command must exit nonzero <strong>at once</strong>, one line on stderr, nothing on stdout — an honest failure, not a hang.</p></li><li><p><strong>One ssh login per bridge connection.</strong> The generated service runs a plain ssh per accepted connection with connection sharing switched off (<code>-o ControlMaster=no -o ControlPath=none -o ControlPersist=no</code>), because sharing hung every bridge on one Windows host. A command-line option wins over <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, so a <code>Control*</code> block there for the host does not reach the relay. A connection lives as long as the frontend session that opened it, so the login is paid once per session.</p></li><li><p><strong>Update ownership is safe to move.</strong> Declaring <code>daemon = true</code> makes the box&#39;s own backend the owner of its updates instead of its frontend&#39;s self-update. Confirm the box&#39;s backend is healthy and at the expected version <em>before</em> step 6, not after.</p></li><li><p><strong>Declare it, one box at a time.</strong> In the hub&#39;s canonical <code>~/.config/sot/hosts.toml</code>:</p><div class="language-toml vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">[</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">host</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">&lt;host&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">daemon = </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">frontend = </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # only if it runs a frontend of its own</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Then on the hub: <code>sotd topology apply</code> (a dry run — read it) and <code>sotd topology apply --yes</code>. It must enable exactly one <code>sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;.socket</code> and disable nothing you did not expect. It also enables <code>sot-relay-tunnel@&lt;host&gt;</code> (the reverse tunnel for messaging) unless the box is a <code>frontend</code>; expect that line. On every other box: <code>sotd topology sync</code>. A box that shares the hub&#39;s home reads the new <code>sotd.service</code> pin at its next boot, or after <code>systemctl --user daemon-reload</code> there; a reload starts nothing, so a box newly declared to run a daemon then needs <code>systemctl --user start sotd</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Verify end to end.</strong> On the hub, <code>sotd status</code> reports that box&#39;s real rows through <code>unix:&lt;runtime&gt;/sot-host-&lt;host&gt;.sock</code>. From another machine, relaunch the frontend: the host appears in Hosts mode, its rows in Sessions mode, attaching to one gives a read-write pty.</p></li></ol><h2 id="Reading-a-failure" tabindex="-1">Reading a failure <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reading-a-failure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading a failure {#Reading-a-failure}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>The socket file exists and the box is down.</strong> Expected, and not a contradiction: systemd owns that socket, so it is present whether or not anything is behind it. The frontend renders the host unreachable and grays its last-known rows. Only a completed hello ever means &quot;up&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>The host stays unreachable and its row&#39;s status text shows an ssh error.</strong> The hub has no listener for the box: step 6 was not applied on the hub, or apply ran while the box was not yet declared.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>sotd topology apply</code> refuses.</strong> It only runs on the hub, and says so, naming the hub. Run it there.</p></li><li><p><strong>The bridge instance fails per connection.</strong> <code>journalctl --user -u &#39;sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;@*&#39;</code> has ssh&#39;s own line: a refused key, an unknown host key, or <code>sotd: command not found</code> (step 1&#39;s check, and the <code>SOT_RELAY_SOTD</code> override).</p></li><li><p><strong>An <code>ExecStart=</code> in a relay drop-in does nothing.</strong> The hub&#39;s <code>zz-sot-relay-command.conf</code> comes after it and sets the command (a later-sorting <code>.conf</code> is renamed to <code>.retired</code> by refresh; an override from another systemd directory is reported as a failure); <code>systemctl --user cat &#39;sot-host-relay-&lt;host&gt;@.service&#39;</code> shows the order.</p></li><li><p><strong>Every remote host went unreachable at once.</strong> That is the hub, not a per-host fault: peers reach every enrolled box <em>through</em> it.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Removing-a-box" tabindex="-1">Removing a box <a class="header-anchor" href="#Removing-a-box" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Removing a box {#Removing-a-box}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Delete its <code>[host.&lt;host&gt;]</code> block on the hub, <code>sotd topology apply --yes</code> (which disables the listener and the box&#39;s <code>sot-relay-tunnel@&lt;host&gt;</code>, and removes the generated files), and <code>sotd topology sync</code> elsewhere. Nothing needs doing on the box itself — the hub was the only side holding a route — unless it shares the hub&#39;s home and runs a daemon from there: then stop it on the box with <code>systemctl --user stop sotd.service</code> and run <code>systemctl --user daemon-reload</code> (<code>enable</code> and <code>disable</code> change no file for <code>sotd.service</code>: its boot start is the shared drop-in <code>default.target.d/sotd.conf</code>, and the pin and <code>hosts.toml</code> decide which hosts start it). The relay sockets and tunnels are not covered: never <code>disable</code> those.</p>`,19)])])}const k=s(n,[["render",a]]);export{g as __pageData,k as default};
